ne expansion from  Alberta to B see that several indigenous  groups still oppose all this at a time  when oil prices are low and some  investors are pulling  as economic recovery unfolds how much  how much net new investment do we see in  the oil and gas sector so I think it's  going to take several years for the oil  and gas sector to get back to its its  pre kovat levels of activity given the  the dramatic economic shock that's  taking place as for Keystone XL  Democratic presidential candidate Joe  Biden has vowed to scrap Donald Trump's  approval of it which means a project now  stopped by the courts is uncertain to  restart after November's US election  Eric Sorenson Global News Toronto still  ahead a bus ride turns tragic for  students in China  you are watching global national crews  in southern Japan are trudging through  knee-deep water trying to rescue people  trapped by flooding at least 55 people  have been killed after days of  torrential rain and landslides and at  least a dozen people are missing over  1.3 million others have been ordered to  leave their homes and more rain is in  the forecast through Thursday in  southwestern China 21 people have been  killed when a bus plunged into a lake  video shows the bus suddenly swerve off  the word road break through a fence and  then plunge into the water the  passengers are believed to have been  high school students on their way to  exams state media says at least 18  people on board were rescued United  Nations investigators say Syria's  pro-government forces and its rebel  opponents flagrantly violated the laws  of war during the battle for it Lib  province the new UN report says  civilians endured what it calls  unfathomable suffering and were subject  to indiscriminate air strikes ground  shelling arrests and torture when  Syria's military launched its deadly  campaign late last year hundreds of  civilians were killed before a ceasefire  was finally agreed in March about 1  million people were displaced and many  have been forced to live in overcrowded  camps  the early bird gets a magnificent sight  next how to spot comet NEOWISE the  pandemic has a lot of us staying home  sometimes without much to do but the  universe is giving us a good reason to  get up early in the morning to see what  many are calling the most impressive  comet in years Mike Armstrong explains  this is the comet NEOWISE as seen over  Switzerland just breaking over the  horizon that's its tail fanned out  behind it now stargazers had two other  comets in 2020 they were looking forward  to both turned out to be disappointments  that is not the case with this one  Neil wise is actually brighter than  expected here it is shot from Burlington  Ontario in the sky over Toronto it's  small but spectacular this photo was  snapped by an astronaut in the space  station this one by an amateur  photographer and Lebanon it's NASA's  official Astronomy Picture of the Day so  about once a decade you get one that is  really bright naked-eye visibility and  that's what surprisingly NEOWISE has  become we weren't expecting it the comet  is named after the NEOWISE spacecraft  the infrared wavelength space telescope  that discovered it it's been visible  with inexpensive ground telescopes and  even binoculars from the southern  hemisphere for a while on its way  towards the Sun as it's gotten closer  it's become easier to see from the  northern hemisphere and brighter it's  rare that comets are so visible with the  naked eye so what is it while comets are  sometimes called dirty snowballs they're  ice and rock and dust the tail is gas  and dust blown off by the sun's heat and  solar wind the glow comes from molecules  breaking down as it gets close to the  Sun you heat it up and that's what  creates the tail now NEOWISE survived  its pass around the Sun last week that's  when most comets disappear this one's  still going it's going to fade as it  moves away from the Sun but not as  quickly as usual because it's getting  closer to Earth the show will continue  for several days to spot it look low on  the horizon to the northeast it's  visible in the early morning now but as  it gets closer to her  it'll be earlier and earlier Mike  Armstrong Global News Montreal and that  is global national for this Tuesday I'm  Donna Friesen tonight's your Canada is  Killarney Ontario beautiful scene thanks  for watching hope to see you here again  tomorrow bye-bye  [Music]
 

